      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "This would also be incredibly more optimized, sync better, and easier to use than the \"particle magic\". We could have working xylophones, keyboards, etc, with very little strain on cpu or net.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2020-07-11T19:22:26.360Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "With avatars 2.0, creators are able to use some sort of particle magic (I never learned to use it myself) to control animations on their avatars. I've seen nice examples with people using it to make \"realistic\" reload for weapons with limited magazine size and the need to pull the magazine far away from the weapon to get it back to full. However, this system depends on particles being enabled (and not hitting particle limits), and is most certainly not synced over network.\nWith that in mind, it would be nice to implement a system for specifying collider/trigger based events that could change parameters on the main avatar animator (either synced or unsynced). The colliders can be handled only locally, so it would not impact performance of remote players.\nA simple example that I could think of is this: let's say an avatar has a pistol in a holster on the hip, and an animation state that would constrain that pistol to avatar's hand. With the proposed system, a completely natural grab interaction can be implemented: a trigger on the holster would set a parameter when a hand enters it, and subsequently triggering the first gesture when that parameter is set would trigger a transition to a state where the pistol is constrained to the hand.\nThis feature would allow for an amazing variety of immersive interactions and controls. A menu is fine, and the only available control method for some things, but natural lifelike control is a strength of VR.",
